  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe will use a multi-grid optimization strategy to solve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. This approach recognizes that optimization performance can be improved by first finding good solutions in coarser grids (which are easier to optimize) and then refining those solutions in finer grids. The rationale is that solutions from larger grids can serve as better starting points for finer grids, potentially leading to higher quality final solutions. This method helps escape local minima that are common in direct optimization of fine-grained representations.\n\nThe core idea is to:\n1. Start from the coarsest available grid (lowest resolution) and record solutions for all grid sizes in a predefined list\n2. For each grid size, use solutions from larger grids (if available) as initial conditions\n3. Perform simulated annealing at each grid, using perturbation and Metropolis criterion\n4. Transfer optimal solutions to finer grids for further refinement\n5. Compare all solutions found across grids and select the one with the lowest score\n\nKey advantages include:\n- Better exploration of the solution space by leveraging multiple scales\n- Faster initial convergence on coarser grids\n- Higher quality final solutions due to multi-step refinement\n- Parallelizable across different grid resolutions \n\nThis approach can significantly improve the initial condition quality found by the standard algorithm and potentially achieve lower C_5 values.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (continuous)\n- Discrete approximation: max_l [correlation(h,1-h,mode='full')] * dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i and \u03a3h_i = n/2\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function with n samples over [0,2] at step size 2/n\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input validation:\n   - Check availability of parent solution (final_h_values if provided)\n   - Set grid size sequence in descending order\n\n2. Template for initial solution construction:\n   - If parent solution available for n=700:\n        use_parent = True\n        start_h = initial_h_values (after validation)\n   - Else:\n        generate random feasible initial solution\n\n3. Core search algorithm (for each grid size n):\n   a. Temperature-based simulated annealing loop:\n      - Initialize temperature and cooling rate (e.g., T=10.0, cooling_rate=0.95)\n      - At each step:\n          1. Perturb current solution (e.g., small Gaussian noise)\n          2. Project to feasibility (0/1 bounds and integral constraint)\n          3. Calculate new objective value\n          4. Acceptance probability: exp(-\u0394C/T) if worse\n      - Update temperature regularly\n\n   b. Time tracking:\n        while time_left > 0 and attempts_remain:\n            perform one cooling cycle\n\n4. Solution transfer between grids:\n   - When n_new > n_old:\n        Find best solution from largest available grid\n        Interpolate to fine grid points using linear interpolation\n        Adjust to enforce feasibility\n\n5. Score evaluation:\n   - After optimization, compute C_5 = max(correlation(h,1-h,mode='full') * dx\n   - Alternatively, use exact integral calculation for validation\n\n6. Final selection:\n   - After processing all grids, select the solution with minimal C_5\n   - Return (h_final, score, n_points) where n_points is the optimal grid size\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Grid sequence: [700, 600, 500, 400, 300] (descending)\n2. Perturbation strategy: \n   - Option 1: Gaussian noise with standard deviation lambda (between 0.1 and 0.5)\n   - Option 2: Random walk with step size alpha\n3. Cooling schedule:\n   - Number of steps: initial T=10 for first 100 steps, then decay\n   - Logarithmic decay: T = T0 / log(t + t_start)\n4. Transfer strategy:\n   - Interpolation: use np.interp for linear interpolation\n   - Adjust: project_feasible to handle feasibility\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n1. Time allocation:\n   - n=700: 35% (385 seconds) assuming main bottleneck\n   - n=600: 25% (275 seconds)\n   - n=500: 20% (220 seconds)\n   - n=400: 10% (110 seconds)\n   - n=300: 10% (110 seconds)\n\n2. Robustness measures:\n   - Query time before each operation\n   - Dynamic step size adjustment based on progress\n   - Exponential backoff on failure\n   - Use fallback solution from previous grid\n\n3. Time estimation:\n   - How to estimate seconds per grid without prior testing?\n   - Use average time proportions for similar n (e.g., time(n) proportional to n*log(n))\n\nValidation and return contract:\nfeasibility_check(h, n):\n   - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n   - |sum(h) - n/2| <= 1e-5 (after project_feasible)\n\nobjective_check(h, n):\n   - Using scipy.optimize.minimize will do feasibility first\n   - Core: ensure score is calculated via compute_c5()\n\ncommon_harness_steps():\n   - The returned strategy must match the expected interface\n   - Use simplex_criterion for determining stopping\n\nFinal validation:\n   - Always evaluate at the grid used\n   - Run compute_c5() on final solution\n   - Ensure solution meets all constraints",
